    "page": 4,
    "text": "Perturbation Theory\n\n• If we increase the mass m of a mass-spring system, will the frequency increase\n  or decrease?\n• Frequency will Decrease\n• If we increase the stiffness k?\n• Frquency will Increase\n• Perturbation Theory:\n      • m ↑↓f\n      • k ↑↑f\n• When we speak, we change the effective mass and stiffness of different portions\n  of air in the vocal tract, differently for each vowel and consonants, so when the\n  air vibrates (due to vocal fold vibration), it vibrates at many frequencies, specific\n  to that V or C, depending on where and how much the air is squeezed!!!!\n• Somehow, for you to hear and interpret the speech, I’m producing, your ear\n needs to figure out what are the low and mid and high frequencies in my voice\n  are, and for the brain to tell, I’ve just heard a high frequency of this much and a\n  low frequency of this much, so he said, e.g., scene, vs. two other frequencies,\n  therefore he said “son”. How?",
